  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 700 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly this morning with areas of fog/low stratus across \nmuch of VA, with patchy frost in the coldest spots along and W\nof I-95. \n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates an amplified pattern with and\nupper level ridge in place from the western Gulf coast to the \nGreat Lakes, and an upper trough offshore. At the surface, ~1030\nmb high pressure is centered nearly overhead, bringing cool\ntemperatures and little to no wind. Patchy ground fog and low \nstratus have developed, and have issued an SPS for locally dense\nfog across southside VA to interior SE VA (but the coverage and\nintensity of fog has is not enough to warrant a Dense Fog\nAdvisory). Temperatures early this morning are still primarily \nin the upper 30s to lower 40s in a saturated airmass so while \nthe Frost Advisory remains in effect for portions of the \npiedmont, conditions look marginal and frost development will \ntend to be patchy and confined to the most sheltered areas as \nmin temperatures will mostly be in the mid to upper 30s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remaining dry through the week. Above normal \n(but still seasonable) temperatures prevail through the end of \nthe week into next weekend. \n\nAfter the cold start, conditions today will be warm with light\nwinds in place ahead of the next front which is still well off \nto the NW of the region today. Expect a mainly sunny sky with \nhighs into the lower 70s inland, and mid to upper 60s along the\ncoast (blended in some of the warmer numerical guidance with the\ncooler NBM). Slightly warmer tonight, though still expect to see\nsome upper 30s in the normally colder spots of rural interior\nsouthern VA where the pressure gradient remains weak and allows\nfor another night with good decoupling. A weak front is forecast\nto drop through the area later Tuesday, shifting winds back to\nthe N-NE with a modest increase in wind speeds along the coast.\nJust a few clouds are expected with near zero chances for rain \nwith this front. High pressure is expected to be centered N of \nthe area Tue night, then become anchored off the southern New\nEngland to mid-Atlantic coast Wed-Thu with low pressure staying\nsuppressed off the SE coast to the Bahamas. The 26/00z ECMWF \nensemble/GEFS chance for measurable rain (.01\"+) of rain through\nthe end of the month is basically zero aside from some low \nprobs showing up in southeastern portions of the area later in \nthe week into the following weekend from the EC ens. (GEFS has \nnone), but when switching the threshold to 0.1\"+ the EC ens. \nbasically has zero probs as well. It should be noted that \ndespite the ongoing drought/precipitation deficit across much of\nthe area, none of our long-term climate sites will rank among \nthe 10 driest Octobers on record (as October has several \noccurrences with monthly rainfall amounts of less than 0.50\").\nTemperatures Wed will be near normal, rising above normal late\nin the week with highs warming well into the 70s.",
